Metal: restore fail-closed async error propagation

0018 added metal_inorder_queue::_async_error_state so that an asynchronous
command-buffer failure would be visible to a later queue wait. 0019 replaced
the device-to-host shared-event path with a synchronous drain and, in doing so,
removed the only writer of that state while leaving the reader in wait().

Net effect at the pinned base: _async_error_state is never written, so
metal_inorder_queue::wait() returns success unconditionally. Kernel command
buffer failures and pointer-translation misses (launch_kernel_from_library),
host-to-device memcpy failures (submit_memcpy) and memset failures
(memset_device) only reached register_error() and could not fail a queue wait.

This restores the 0018 guarantee for every surviving completion handler:

- Move async_error_state out of metal_inorder_queue's private section to
  namespace scope as metal_async_error_state, so the submission helpers that
  install completion handlers can name it.
- Add record_metal_async_failure(), which latches the first failure and still
  forwards to register_error().
- Thread the shared state into launch_kernel_from_library() and
  memset_device(), and capture it in the submit_memcpy() completion handler.

The state is held by shared_ptr and captured by value because completion
handlers may outlive the queue that submitted them. The latched error is
sticky: once a failure is recorded, subsequent waits keep reporting it.

Device-to-host memcpy is unaffected; 0019 made it return errors directly.

